        [
          "python",
          "Example test dataset for a summarization prompt:\n\nTest 1 - Common case:\nInput: [News article, 800 words]\nExpected: Summary 100-150 words, includes headline, key points\nCriteria: Accuracy (claims match article), Completeness (all major points), Clarity\n\nTest 2 - Edge case (very long):\nInput: [Technical paper, 5000 words, dense with equations]\nExpected: Handles complex content, maintains technical accuracy\nCriteria: Accuracy of technical claims, Selectivity (chooses most important points)\n\nTest 3 - Edge case (very short):\nInput: [Tweet, 140 characters]\nExpected: Doesn't over-summarize, preserves essence\nCriteria: Doesn't lose meaning, reasonable length\n\nTest 4 - Adversarial:\nInput: [Article with contradictory statements designed to confuse]\nExpected: Identifies contradictions or notes main position\nCriteria: Doesn't confidently present contradictions as facts\n\nTest 5 - Diverse domains:\nInput: [Medical article, Legal document, Sports report]\nExpected: Domain-appropriate summaries\nCriteria: Accuracy (domain-specific fact checking)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Evaluation rubric for summarization:\n\nAccuracy (1-5 scale):\n- 5: All claims in summary are accurate and well-supported by source\n- 4: Summary is mostly accurate with minor inaccuracies or unsupported claims\n- 3: Mix of accurate and inaccurate claims\n- 2: Most claims unsupported or inaccurate\n- 1: Summary contains significant false information\n\nCompleteness (1-5 scale):\n- 5: Covers all major points from source\n- 4: Covers most major points, misses one minor point\n- 3: Covers some major points, misses several\n- 2: Covers few major points\n- 1: Misses most major points\n\nClarity (1-5 scale):\n- 5: Very clear and well-organized\n- 4: Clear with minor organization issues\n- 3: Generally understandable but could be clearer\n- 2: Hard to follow in places\n- 1: Very confusing or incoherent\n\nConciseness (1-5 scale):\n- 5: Concise, no unnecessary information\n- 4: Mostly concise with minor verbosity\n- 3: Some unnecessary information but mostly on-target\n- 2: Quite verbose, includes non-essential details\n- 1: Very verbose, many redundant details\n\nOverall Score: Average of four dimensions"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "from nltk.translate.bleu_score import sentence_bleu\n\nreference = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\nhypothesis = \"The quick brown fox jumps over a lazy dog\"\n\n# BLEU score (1-gram precision)\nscore = sentence_bleu([reference.split()], hypothesis.split())\nprint(f\"BLEU Score: {score:.4f}\")  # Higher is better\n\n# Interpretation:\n# BLEU measures n-gram overlap between generated and reference text\n# Range: 0-1 (though rarely reaches 1)\n# Good for translation, summarization evaluation"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "from rouge_score import rouge_scorer\n\nreference = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\nhypothesis = \"The quick fox jumps over the dog\"\n\nscorer = rouge_scorer.RougeScorer(['rouge1', 'rougeL'], use_stemmer=True)\nscores = scorer.score(reference, hypothesis)\n\nprint(f\"ROUGE-1: {scores['rouge1'].fmeasure:.4f}\")  # F-measure\nprint(f\"ROUGE-L: {scores['rougeL'].fmeasure:.4f}\")\n\n# ROUGE-1: Unigram overlap\n# ROUGE-L: Longest common subsequence\n# Higher scores indicate better summary quality"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "from bert_score import score\n\nreference = \"The summary discusses climate change and its impacts on agriculture.\"\nhypothesis = \"Climate change is affecting farming and agricultural practices.\"\n\nP, R, F1 = score([hypothesis], [reference], lang='en', verbose=True)\n\nprint(f\"Precision: {P.item():.4f}\")  # How much of hypothesis is relevant\nprint(f\"Recall: {R.item():.4f}\")    # How much of reference is captured\nprint(f\"F1 Score: {F1.item():.4f}\") # Harmonic mean\n\n# BERTScore uses embeddings to measure semantic similarity\n# Better than BLEU/ROUGE for semantic equivalence\n# Handles paraphrasing and synonyms better"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "import json\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\ndef run_ab_test(test_cases, prompt_a, prompt_b, model):\n    \"\"\"\n    Run A/B test comparing two prompts.\n    Returns: results dict with statistics\n    \"\"\"\n    results_a = []\n    results_b = []\n    \n    for test_case in test_cases:\n        user_input = test_case['input']\n        \n        # Get responses\n        response_a = model.generate(prompt=prompt_a + \"\\n\\n\" + user_input)\n        response_b = model.generate(prompt=prompt_b + \"\\n\\n\" + user_input)\n        \n        # Evaluate (using human or automated metrics)\n        score_a = evaluate_response(response_a, test_case['reference'])\n        score_b = evaluate_response(response_b, test_case['reference'])\n        \n        results_a.append({'input': user_input, 'response': response_a, 'score': score_a})\n        results_b.append({'input': user_input, 'response': response_b, 'score': score_b})\n    \n    # Aggregate results\n    avg_a = sum(r['score'] for r in results_a) / len(results_a)\n    avg_b = sum(r['score'] for r in results_b) / len(results_b)\n    \n    # Statistical significance (simplified)\n    from scipy import stats\n    scores_a = [r['score'] for r in results_a]\n    scores_b = [r['score'] for r in results_b]\n    t_stat, p_value = stats.ttest_ind(scores_a, scores_b)\n    \n    return {\n        'prompt_a_avg': avg_a,\n        'prompt_b_avg': avg_b,\n        'improvement': ((avg_b - avg_a) / avg_a) * 100,  # % improvement\n        'p_value': p_value,\n        'significant': p_value < 0.05,\n        'winner': 'B' if avg_b > avg_a else 'A'\n    }\n\n# Example\ntest_cases = [\n    {'input': 'Summarize this article...', 'reference': 'Expected summary'},\n    # ... more test cases\n]\n\nresults = run_ab_test(test_cases, prompt_v1, prompt_v2, model)\nprint(f\"Prompt A average: {results['prompt_a_avg']:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Prompt B average: {results['prompt_b_avg']:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Improvement: {results['improvement']:.1f}%\")\nprint(f\"Statistically significant: {results['significant']}\")\nprint(f\"Winner: Prompt {results['winner']}\")"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
